WEBVTT

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.140
Can we implement them using Python?

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.520
Of course, infinitely many.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:26.540
And the same is true of any programming language, for example, in C, in Java,

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:35.740
or OCaml, in any language, we can implement many— I mean, an infinite set of programs.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:46.950
Which means, defining a programming language is—sorry, designing a programming language is

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.850
equivalent

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.280
to defining an infinite set.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:11.330
And in today's class, inductive definition is, I think, the one and only

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.420
way to define an infinite set formally.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:21.520
That's why I told you that the first question is really important in my last lecture.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:27.760
So before we learn this, please, I have a quiz.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.220
Let's try this.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:41.100
Defining programs right now is very difficult, but you can do this already. For example, define

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.220
the set of

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:45.800
multiples of 3.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.730
This is important, so let me repeat: here you'll learn how to design

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:07.620
a programming language. What does designing a language mean? It means defining an infinite set.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.950
With a single programming language, we can implement infinitely many

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.020
programs. So designing a programming language is the same as defining this infinite set.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:30.880
And what I'm introducing today is almost the only method we humans have for formally defining an infinite set.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.370
Before learning it, let's try it with infinite sets we already know.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.420
It's something you can do.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:44.300
What I'm asking you now is to formally define multiples of 3. We haven't learned it yet, but you might already know.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:45.620
Let's just give it a try.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.910
Let's start from 0, for example. In CS, 0 counts as a natural number.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.760
No questions on that.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.280
0, 3, 6, 9...

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.140
These dots are informal.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:12.350
They're not precisely defined. Write it so that anyone would

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.770
agree it expresses exactly the multiples of 3, formally.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.230
Try it. You know what multiples of 3 are. How would you

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.070
put that formally? Let's try. We haven't learned it, but think.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.640
I'll give you about 15 minutes.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.780
But not this one—you could also do it this way.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.910
Not this one. Anyway, today

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.050
there are various inductive definitions, so use a different

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:52.000
approach for multiples of 3. We haven't learned it, but think and write it down.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:59.380
I'll give you about 15 minutes. Later, it'll be fun to check whether your idea matches what we learn afterward.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:23.150
If you just listen without trying, you'll forget the rest right away. Trying it yourself makes it stick much longer.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:24.860
That's why I'm asking you to try it.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.720
That 0 is a multiple of 3.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.720
Oh, yes. You mean the base case.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:32.910
We define the base case of...

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:33.870
We define the base case.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:34.920
Yeah.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:43.540
Let's say we're defining a set S, then you're saying 0 is an element of S, right? Yeah, and then?

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:53.650
Also, we have to agree that if X is an element of S, X plus 3 is also an element of S.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:05:00.310
Oh, you mean, if X is an element of S,

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:05.920
that implies X plus 3 is also an element of S, right?

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.380
Who thinks it's the correct definition?

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:19.960
And I think you already know a bottom-up inductive definition.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.020
Have you seen this?

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:26.000
Or did you work it out without seeing this? - I just figured it out.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.120
Oh, really?

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.460
He just used the bottom-up definition to define multiples of 3.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:42.720
The concept of bottom-up inductive definition is about constructing sets from a small set toward an infinite set.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.830
For example, first we define the base elements.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.070
For example, 0 is the base element in multiples of 3.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:56.720
So at this point, we first define that 0 is an element of S.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.980
And then we define the inductive case.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:05.180
If N is an element of S, then N plus 3 is also an element of S.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:07.740
It is formal.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.770
There's no doubt that S is the set of multiples of 3. But I think we need to think about this.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.640
I think he missed this point.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:19.940
How about this?

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.740
S should be the smallest set, I guess.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:28.640
Great.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.840
So, now let's see.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:35.920
Let's look at inductive definition,

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:44.290
which is about formally defining an infinite set. So, inductive definition is widely used in

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.990
every computer science domain, including programming languages.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.390
It's also widely used in data

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:58.550
structures. For example, when we define any data structure, say, lists, trees, or graphs, these data

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.680
structures are defined inductively.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.080
They have to be.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:10.700
So, conceptually, inductive definition is a tool for defining sets formally.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:21.220
And the key characteristic of inductive definition is that when you define a set, the definition uses the set itself.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:32.470
One of the biggest features of induction: we're defining S here, right? The key feature of inductive definition is that

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.200
we use S when defining S.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.730
We're defining something, using the very thing we're defining to define that set.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:48.460
Other than this, there's no way to define an infinite set with finite sentences, as far as I know.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:50.380
There's no other way.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:59.890
I asked you to try in case someone among you had another brilliant idea. So, does anyone

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:05.960
think, 'I know a way other than inductive definition'? Or 'I just did it'?

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:09.340
It wasn't easy, was it?

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.260
Anyway, that's how it is. So currently, inductive definition is

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.720
the only way to formally define an infinite set.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.620
This is the only way to define an infinite set by finite means.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:25.830
That's why it is important.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:30.610
And today, we have three styles of inductive definitions.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.180
The first is called top-down style inductive definition.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:42.570
Intuitively, top-down inductive definition describes a set by describing the properties of its elements.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:49.810
For example, intuitively, top-down inductive definition describes properties

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:57.940
of each element.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:04.610
In Korean, for example, top-down feels like this.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:08.510
When defining an infinite set, we describe the properties

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.330
its elements must satisfy, and anything satisfying them

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:18.030
is an element of our set. E.g., to define the set of men, biologically,

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.200
the chromosomes are XY— that's describing a property.

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:29.700
Defining a set by its properties like this is called top-down style inductive definition.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:34.830
Describing properties the elements satisfy is top-down style,

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:42.090
and in bottom-up style inductive definition, like he did, we construct an infinite set from a base

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:43.980
case toward the infinite case.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:53.290
That's how, intuitively, bottom-up style inductive definition constructs sets

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.600
from zero to infinity.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:03.640
And also, we can define a set using inductive rules of inference.

